{"id":"e02t4","name":"Backorder Simulator","lang":"python","entry":"solution","params":["events"],"limit_s":4,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>A warehouse wants to replay one day of activity to understand which orders waited for stock. You are given a list of events <code>events</code>, in the order they happened. Each event is a string in one of these formats:</p>\n<ul><li><code>\"restock &lt;sku&gt; &lt;qty&gt;\"</code> - <code>qty</code> units of <code>sku</code> arrive;</li><li><code>\"order &lt;order_id&gt; &lt;sku&gt; &lt;qty&gt;\"</code> - a customer orders <code>qty</code> units of <code>sku</code>;</li><li><code>\"cancel &lt;order_id&gt;\"</code> - the customer cancels an order.</li></ul>\n<p>The stock of every SKU starts at <code>0</code>. Events are processed one by one, and the event index starts at <code>0</code>:</p>\n<ol><li>an order is <b>filled</b> immediately if no earlier order for the same SKU is waiting and the stock is enough; the stock decreases by <code>qty</code>. Otherwise the order <b>waits</b> at the end of the queue of its SKU;</li><li>after a restock, the queue of that SKU is served in order: while the first waiting order can be filled, it is filled (at the index of the restock event) and removed from the queue. An order that cannot be filled blocks the orders behind it;</li><li>a cancel removes a waiting order from its queue, and it becomes <b>cancelled</b> at the index of the cancel event. After a cancel, the queue of that SKU is served again as in rule 2, at the same index. Cancelling an order that is already filled or cancelled, or that does not exist, does nothing.</li></ol>\n<p>Return one entry per order, in the order the orders appear in <code>events</code>: <code>[order_id, status, index]</code>, where <code>status</code> is <code>\"filled\"</code>, <code>\"cancelled\"</code> or <code>\"waiting\"</code> (still waiting at the end of the day), and <code>index</code> is the index of the event that filled or cancelled it, or <code>-1</code> if it is still waiting.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4><p>For <code>events = [\"restock A 5\", \"order o1 A 4\", \"order o2 A 3\", \"order o3 A 1\", \"restock A 4\", \"order o4 B 1\"]</code>, the output should be</p><pre class=\"exv\">[[\"o1\", \"filled\", 1], [\"o2\", \"filled\", 4], [\"o3\", \"filled\", 4], [\"o4\", \"waiting\", -1]]</pre>\n<p><code>o2</code> waits because only 1 unit of <code>A</code> is left. <code>o3</code> waits behind <code>o2</code> even if 1 unit would be enough. The restock at index 4 fills <code>o2</code> and then <code>o3</code>.</p><div class=\"lim\"><b>[execution time limit]</b> 4 seconds (py3)<br><b>[memory limit]</b> 1 GB<br><b>[input]</b> array.string events<br><b>[output]</b> array.array</div>","starter":"def solution(events):\n    # write your code here\n    return []\n","hints":[],"summary_fr":"","tags":["simulation","file FIFO","deque","parsing"],"minutes":25,"source":"Kata Lab","howto":"<h5>Où écrire</h5><ol><li>Écris dans l'éditeur à droite, fichier <code>solution.py</code>.</li><li>Complète la fonction <code>solution(events)</code>. Garde son nom et ses paramètres : ce sont les tests qui l'appellent, avec leurs propres données. N'écris pas d'appel à la fonction toi-même.</li></ol><h5>Ce que tu dois renvoyer</h5><ol><li>Ta fonction doit <b>renvoyer</b> le résultat avec <code>return</code>. Un <code>print</code> ne compte pas : il sort seulement dans l'onglet <b>Console Output</b>.</li><li>Ici, la valeur attendue est une liste : compare ce que tu renvoies à « Expected return value » dans les résultats.</li><li>La comparaison est exacte (même type, mêmes valeurs, même ordre dans une liste), sauf les nombres décimaux, comparés à 0,000001 près.</li></ol><h5>Lancer et vérifier</h5><ol><li>Clique <b>RUN TESTS</b> (ou <code>Ctrl + Entrée</code>) : tes 2 tests visibles tournent.</li><li>Quand c'est bon, tu vois en vert <b>« Tests passed: 2/2. »</b>. Sinon, ouvre le test marqué <b>Wrong answer</b> : compare « Expected return value » et « Your return value », et clique <b>VIEW DIFF</b> pour colorer les cases différentes. <b>Runtime error</b> veut dire que ton code a planté : le message est dans le test.</li><li>Clique <b>SUBMIT</b> : ta note sur 100 compte aussi 10 tests cachés. Soumets dès que quelques tests passent, puis améliore et resoumets : c'est la dernière soumission qui compte.</li></ol>","files":null,"visible":[{"input":{"events":["restock A 5","order o1 A 4","order o2 A 3","order o3 A 1","restock A 4","order o4 B 1"]},"expected":{"value":[["o1","filled",1],["o2","filled",4],["o3","filled",4],["o4","waiting",-1]]}},{"input":{"events":["restock A 1","order x A 5","order y A 1","cancel x","cancel x","cancel zz"]},"expected":{"value":[["x","cancelled",3],["y","filled",3]]}}],"hidden_n":10,"hidden":"","solution":""}